.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt BSDT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bsdt
.Nd repeatable FreeBSD development VMs from a TOML file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Ar command
.Op Ar args
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
boots a FreeBSD virtual machine for the project in the current directory,
described by a
.Pa bsdt.toml
environment file, much as
.Ql docker compose up
starts containers from a compose file.
It is meant for building and testing software on FreeBSD while working on
Linux or macOS.
.Pp
The guest is booted with
.Xr qemu-system-x86_64 1
or
.Xr qemu-system-aarch64 1
from the official FreeBSD
.Sy BASIC-CLOUDINIT
VM image, which is downloaded once, checked against the release's
.Pa CHECKSUM.SHA256
and cached.
Each project gets its own copy-on-write overlay disk on top of the cached
image, so a VM can be thrown away and recreated in seconds.
.Pp
On first boot, a small configuration disk is attached that FreeBSD's
.Xr nuageinit 7
reads to create a
.Ql bsdt
user and to allow key-based SSH logins as that user and as root.
.Nm
then installs the listed packages, copies the project into the guest with
.Xr rsync 1
and runs the provision commands.
Later commands reach the guest over SSH on a forwarded port bound to
127.0.0.1.
.Pp
Hardware acceleration is used when the guest architecture matches the
host: KVM on Linux and the Hypervisor framework on macOS.
Otherwise QEMU emulates the CPU, which works but is much slower.
.Pp
NetBSD and OpenBSD guests are planned.
They are accepted in the environment file but rejected by
.Cm up
for now.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm init
Write a commented starter
.Pa bsdt.toml
in the current directory, or to the path given with
.Fl f .
.It Cm up
Boot the VM.
On first use this downloads the base image if needed, creates the
overlay disk, waits for first-boot setup to finish, installs packages,
syncs the project and runs the provision commands.
On later runs it boots the existing disk and syncs.
Does nothing if the VM is already running.
.It Cm down
Shut the guest down through ACPI, waiting up to a minute before stopping
QEMU forcibly.
The disk is kept.
.It Cm destroy
Stop QEMU immediately and delete the VM's state directory, including its
disk.
The cached base image is kept.
.It Cm status
Show the environment file, guest, sync destination, whether the VM is
running, and its forwarded ports.
.It Cm ssh Op Fl -root
Open a login shell in the guest directory matching the current directory.
.It Cm exec Oo Fl -root Oc Oo Fl -no-sync Oc Fl - Ar command Op Ar arg ...
Sync the project, then run
.Ar command
in the guest directory matching the current directory.
Each argument is passed through unchanged, so shell syntax needs an
explicit
.Ql sh -c .
A terminal is allocated when standard input and output are terminals.
The exit status is that of
.Ar command .
.It Cm sync
Copy the project directory into the guest; see
.Sx SYNCING .
.It Cm provision
Install the packages and run the provision commands again on the running
VM.
.It Cm gui
Open a VNC viewer window on the VM's desktop; see
.Sx DESKTOP .
.Cm up
does this already unless
.Ic gui.open
is false, so this is for reopening a closed window.
.It Cm key Ar chord ...
Press each
.Ar chord
in turn on the VM's keyboard, as if typed on it: the keys go through the
emulated USB keyboard, so the guest sees them in
.Pa /dev/input .
A chord is key names joined by
.Ql +
or
.Ql - ,
pressed in order and released in reverse, such as
.Ql super+return
or
.Ql ctrl-alt-f2 .
Names are QEMU's key codes, such as
.Ql a ,
.Ql 1 ,
.Ql f5 ,
.Ql tab ,
.Ql slash
or
.Ql minus ,
plus the aliases
.Ql super
.Pq also Ql meta , Ql win , Ql cmd ,
.Ql ctrl ,
.Ql alt ,
.Ql shift ,
.Ql enter ,
.Ql space ,
.Ql escape
and
.Ql plus .
Needs
.Ic vm.input ,
which
.Ic vm.gui
turns on.
.It Cm type Oo Fl -enter Oc Ar text
Type
.Ar text
on the VM's keyboard, one key at a time, assuming a US layout.
With
.Fl -enter ,
press Return afterwards.
.It Cm logs Op Fl F
Print the guest's serial console log, which shows the boot and is the
first place to look when
.Cm up
times out.
.It Cm pull
Download and cache the base image for the environment file without
booting anything.
.It Cm images
List cached base images and their sizes.
.It Cm man Op Fl -install
Print this manual page to standard output.
With
.Fl -install ,
write it to
.Pa ../share/man/man1/bsdt.1
relative to the directory holding the
.Nm
binary instead, and print the path written.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f , Fl -file Ar file
Use
.Ar file
as the environment file instead of the first
.Pa bsdt.toml
found in the current directory or its parents.
Each environment file gets its own VM, so one project can have several.
.It Fl -root
For
.Cm ssh
and
.Cm exec ,
log in as root instead of the
.Ql bsdt
user.
.It Fl -no-sync
For
.Cm exec ,
skip syncing the project first.
.It Fl -enter
For
.Cm type ,
press Return after the text.
.It Fl F , Fl -follow
For
.Cm logs ,
keep printing as the log grows.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a short usage summary.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT FILE
The environment file is TOML.
Only
.Ic vm.os
and
.Ic vm.version
are required.
.Ss [vm]
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic os
The guest operating system.
Only
.Ql freebsd
is supported so far.
.It Ic version
The release to boot, such as
.Ql 15.1
or
.Ql 14.5 .
.It Ic arch
.Ql auto
(the default) to match the host,
.Ql amd64
or
.Ql aarch64 .
.It Ic cpus
Number of virtual CPUs.
Defaults to 2.
.It Ic memory
Memory size in QEMU syntax.
Defaults to
.Ql 2G .
.It Ic disk
Size of the overlay disk; the guest grows its file system to fill it on
first boot.
Defaults to
.Ql 20G .
.It Ic filesystem
.Ql ufs
(the default) or
.Ql zfs ,
choosing between the two images FreeBSD publishes.
.It Ic hostname
The guest's hostname.
Defaults to the project directory's name.
.It Ic update
When true, let the image install FreeBSD security updates on first boot
and reboot, as it does by default outside
.Nm .
Defaults to false, so every VM starts from exactly the release image and
the first
.Cm up
is much faster.
Only read when the disk is created.
.It Ic gui
When true, run a desktop in the guest and open a window on it; see
.Sx DESKTOP .
Defaults to false.
.It Ic audio
When true, give the guest an Intel HDA sound card that plays through the
host's sound server, and load
.Xr snd_hda 4
for it on every boot, so it appears as
.Pa /dev/dsp .
.Nm
uses the best audio backend QEMU was built with: PipeWire, PulseAudio,
ALSA, SDL or OSS on Linux, and Core Audio or SDL on macOS.
There is no microphone.
Defaults to false.
.It Ic input
When true, attach a USB keyboard and tablet, which the guest sees as
evdev devices in
.Pa /dev/input ,
and which
.Cm key
and
.Cm type
press keys on.
Defaults to the value of
.Ic gui .
.It Ic ports
TCP ports to forward from 127.0.0.1 on the host, as
.Ql HOST:GUEST
strings, or a single port number when both sides are the same.
.El
.Ss [packages]
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic install
Packages to install with
.Xr pkg 8 .
.Xr rsync 1
is always installed as well, since
.Nm
needs it to sync.
.El
.Ss [sync]
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic dest
Absolute guest path to sync the project to.
Defaults to
.Pa /home/bsdt/ Ns Ar name ,
where
.Ar name
is the project directory's name.
.It Ic exclude
.Xr rsync 1
exclude patterns, such as build output directories.
.El
.Ss [provision]
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic root
Shell commands run as root after packages are installed.
.It Ic run
Shell commands run as the
.Ql bsdt
user in the synced directory, after
.Ic root .
.El
.Pp
Provision commands run in order on the first
.Cm up
and on every
.Cm provision ,
and stop at the first failure.
.Ss [gui]
Only read when
.Ic vm.gui
is true.
Every key has a default, so the table can be left out.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic desktop
.Ql sway
(the default) or
.Ql none .
With
.Ql sway ,
.Nm
installs and runs the desktop described in
.Sx DESKTOP .
With
.Ql none ,
it runs only
.Ic start ,
which must bring up something serving VNC on
.Ic vnc .
.It Ic start
A shell command run as the
.Ql bsdt
user in the synced directory once the desktop is up, on every boot.
With
.Ql sway ,
it runs inside the desktop, and defaults to
.Ql foot ,
a terminal.
An empty string runs nothing.
.It Ic vnc
Port the guest's VNC server listens on.
Defaults to 5900.
.It Ic port
Host port on 127.0.0.1 forwarded to
.Ic vnc .
Defaults to a free port picked at each
.Cm up ,
which
.Cm status
shows.
.It Ic open
Whether
.Cm up
opens a viewer window.
Defaults to true.
.It Ic resolution
Size of the desktop, as
.Ar width Ns Li x Ns Ar height .
Defaults to
.Ql 1280x800 .
.It Ic modifier
Sway's modifier key in the configuration
.Nm
generates:
.Ql alt
(the default) or
.Ql super .
Desktops on the host usually keep Super combinations for themselves
while the viewer is in a window, so Alt reaches the VM more reliably.
.It Ic fullscreen
Open the TigerVNC viewer full screen, where it passes system keys such as
Super and Alt+Tab to the VM instead of the host.
Defaults to false.
.El
.Sh DESKTOP
FreeBSD has no driver for QEMU's virtual graphics cards, so a desktop
cannot draw to the VM's own screen.
Instead, with
.Ic vm.gui
set,
.Nm
runs
.Xr sway 1
on a headless output, drawn in software, and shares it over VNC with
.Xr wayvnc 1 .
Input still comes from the VM's USB keyboard and tablet through
.Sy libinput
and
.Xr seatd 1 .
.Pp
The first
.Cm up
installs
.Sy sway ,
.Sy seatd ,
.Sy wayvnc ,
.Sy foot
and
.Sy dejavu ,
enables
.Xr seatd 1 ,
and adds the
.Ql bsdt
user to the
.Ql video
group.
Every
.Cm up
then starts sway, wayvnc and
.Ic gui.start ,
waits for the VNC server to answer, and opens a viewer on the forwarded
port.
On macOS that is always the TigerVNC viewer
.Pq Ql brew install --cask tigervnc-viewer ,
since the built-in Screen Sharing requires a password and the desktop has
none.
On other systems it is the first of
.Xr vncviewer 1 ,
.Xr xtigervncviewer 1 ,
.Xr remmina 1
and
.Xr krdc 1
that is installed, or whatever
.Xr xdg-open 1
opens
.Ql vnc://
links with.
.Pp
Sway reads
.Pa ~/.config/sway/config
in the guest.
When that file does not exist, or still starts with the comment
.Nm
writes,
.Nm
generates it from sway's stock configuration with
.Ic gui.modifier
as the modifier, so
.Ql alt+return
opens a terminal by default.
Delete the comment to keep your own edits.
Its output and wayvnc's go to
.Pa /tmp/bsdt-sway.log
and
.Pa /tmp/bsdt-wayvnc.log
in the guest.
.Pp
Keys typed into the VNC viewer reach sway through wayvnc, not through
.Pa /dev/input .
Programs that read input devices directly, such as hotkey daemons, only
see keys sent with
.Cm key
and
.Cm type .
.Sh SYNCING
The directory holding the environment file is mirrored to
.Ic sync.dest
as the
.Ql bsdt
user by
.Cm up ,
.Cm sync
and
.Cm exec .
Files deleted on the host are deleted in the guest, except for paths
matching
.Ic sync.exclude ,
which are neither copied nor deleted, so build output in the guest
survives a sync.
The
.Pa .bsdt
directory is always excluded.
.Pp
Syncing is one way, from host to guest.
Use
.Xr scp 1
or
.Cm exec
to get results back.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev BSDT_CACHE_DIR
Cache directory to use instead of the default.
.It Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
When set, the cache is
.Pa $XDG_CACHE_HOME/bsdt .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa bsdt.toml
The environment file.
.It Pa .bsdt/ Ns Ar stem Ns Pa /
Per-VM state beside the environment file, where
.Ar stem
is the environment file's name without
.Pa .toml :
the overlay disk
.Pa disk.qcow2 ,
the configuration disk
.Pa seed.img ,
the SSH key pair
.Pa id_ed25519 ,
the serial console log
.Pa console.log ,
QEMU's pid file
.Pa qemu.pid
and the forwarded ports in
.Pa state.json .
It contains a
.Pa .gitignore
that ignores everything in it.
.It Pa ~/.cache/bsdt/images/
Cached base images on Linux;
.Pa ~/Library/Caches/bsdt/images/
on macOS.
Delete files here to free space.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Cm ssh
and
.Cm exec
exit with the status of the remote shell or command.
Otherwise,
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start a project with a FreeBSD 15.1 VM that has Rust installed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bsdt init
$ $EDITOR bsdt.toml
$ bsdt up
.Ed
.Pp
with a
.Pa bsdt.toml
like:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[vm]
os = "freebsd"
version = "15.1"
memory = "4G"
ports = ["8080"]

[packages]
install = ["rust", "git"]

[sync]
exclude = ["target"]

[provision]
run = ["cargo fetch"]
.Ed
.Pp
Build and test after editing on the host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bsdt exec -- cargo test
.Ed
.Pp
Run a pipeline, which needs a shell in the guest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bsdt exec -- sh -c 'uname -a | tee uname.txt'
.Ed
.Pp
Keep a second VM for an older release next to the default one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cp bsdt.toml freebsd14.toml
$ sed -i.bak 's/15.1/14.5/' freebsd14.toml
$ bsdt -f freebsd14.toml up
.Ed
.Pp
Open a sway desktop, then drive it from a script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cat bsdt.toml
[vm]
os = "freebsd"
version = "15.1"
gui = true
$ bsdt up
$ bsdt type --enter 'uname -a'
$ bsdt key alt+return
.Ed
.Pp
Start again from a clean disk:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bsdt destroy && bsdt up
.Ed
.Pp
Install this page after
.Ql cargo install bsdt ,
or read it without installing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bsdt man --install
$ bsdt man | man -l -
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr qemu-img 1 ,
.Xr rsync 1 ,
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr nuageinit 7
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Divan Visagie Aq Mt me@divanv.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Programs that use ALSA, rather than FreeBSD's own OSS interface, need the
.Sy alsa-plugins
package to reach the sound card.
.Pp
The desktop's VNC server has no password.
Like SSH, it is only reachable through a port bound to 127.0.0.1 on the
host.
.Pp
The guest's root account has no password on the serial console, as in
the official images, and accepts the project's SSH key.
The VM is meant for development, not for anything exposed to a network.
